  "notes": "The lane's harmonist-in-chief on the jazz side — the *Black Radio* project made jazz-piano voicings the default modern soul harmony. The entry calls the harmony \"**the parameter to copy**\": he \"learned his own way to hear harmony and was inspired to mix church and gospel harmonies with jazz harmonies\", giving \"gospel plagal and deceptive cadences voiced with jazz extensions, upper-structure triads over unrelated bass notes, quartal left-hand shapes, and **systematic reharmonisation of a standard on each pass** — expect the same 4-bar melody supported by three different chord sets across a song\". A fixed progression family is the one thing that cannot express systematic reharmonisation, so none is authored; instead the triad weight is 0.02, the ninth share is 0.62, `susSubstitutionProb` is 0.4 for the quartal shapes and the chord durations are short. \"Upper-structure triads over unrelated bass notes\" is why `followRootsProb` on the bass is 0.24 and the chord voicing had to start at 52. Drums are inherited from his working drummer: \"his working drummer is Chris Dave, so the displaced-snare/ghost-note grammar above is the default kit. On the acoustic-trio material the kit is swung and ride-led with an implied rather than stated backbeat\" — hence a 12–26 ms displacement, a 0.68 ghost probability, a hat weighted toward 8ths and mostly withheld, and ride and ride-bell as the busiest lanes. Bass is \"Derrick Hodge's played electric bass — melodic, chord-tone-driven, moving with the reharmonisation, occasionally distorted. No 808.\" The third melodic voice is named — \"Casey Benjamin's vocoder and saxophone… the vocoder-as-harmony-instrument is a signature timbre of this lane's 2012+ wing\" — which is the sustain-pad counter. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every drum number and all melody figures; the research marks tempo detail as inferred.",
